A1.3 – Return More Than One Value (2 Marks)
Aim: To return both the number of eligible donors and the ID of the youngest eligible donor.
Alternative 1: Using Pointer Parameters
In this method, the function returns the number of eligible donors and uses a pointer parameter to return the youngest donor's ID.

int youngestID;
int count = findYoungestEligible(donors, n, "O+", &youngestID);

printf("Eligible donors: %d\n", count);
printf("Youngest donor ID: %d\n", youngestID);

Comparison of the Two Alternatives
Feature Pointer Parameters Structure Return
Number of values returned Return value + pointer Multiple fields in structure
Readability Requires understanding pointers Easy to understand
Safety Caller must provide a valid pointer No output pointer needed
Extensibility More output pointers may be needed Easy to add more result fields
Best use Simple functions with few outputs Functions with multiple related outputs
Conclusion: Returning a structure is preferred when a function must return multiple related values because it improves readability, maintainability and extensibility.

